Bottom-up research is another approach you can use to understand a particular engineering domain and find your way to project ideas.

Broadly, bottom-up research begins with a specific product or tool and asks why does this exist? What development challenge was it built to solve?

By answering that question, we can 'broaden' our lens to the specific engineering subproblem that this tool solves, and ask "how does a developer arrive at this particular problem? What were trying to solve, such that they arrived at this tool? "

We can then explore that broader engineering domain. How did the developer team arrive at this problem space? Define what decisions and tradeoffs an engineering team would have made to arrive at this domain.

In this way, we work our way "up" from a highly specific subproblem to increasingly general engineering domains, investigating the various decisions and alternatives that must be made along the way to our specific project.

Bottom-up research example
While researching engineering blogs, you find an interesting article on a tool called Koi Pond (https://slack.engineering/load-testing-with-koi-pond/)
Summarize the key points of the article, including the context on what problem it was solving at Slack: load testing of websocket connections
We now have three broader domains to research: what is load testing, what are websockets, and why when would we need to load test websockets?
Research the domain of load testing. Learn about the various types of load testing
Why would a team need to load test? What are their options in load testing tools?
Research the domain of websockets?
What is the use case for websockets? What are the various alternatives to websockets?
Why would you need to load test websocket connections?
One of the possibilities here is if you have a real-time collaboration app
What is a real-time collaboration? What are the characteristics of it? What architectural decisions would you need to make to build an app with real-time collaboration?

See the first 1/3 of the Edamame case study: https://get-edamame.com/case-study. The Edamame team did their research as a bottom-up process (though their case study is written in a typical top-down fashion).

Conducting bottom-up research
We would like each team to practice bottom-up research as part of their ideation process
Begin by identifying an interesting tool or project
Research the tool's use case. You can use the tool's own website or associated engineering blog, but also look to other resources like 3rd-party articles, blogs, and forum posts about the tool.
Aim to answer the questions: what engineering problem does this solve? What engineering domain does this belong to? What set of problems would an engineer have, such that this tool would be the solution?
After you've identified the use case for the tool, research the more general engineering subdomain(s) that the tool belongs to. How would a team arrive at this particular subdomain? What would be the characteristics of their application? What alternatives to your tool might they have considered? What broader engineering domain does this subdomain belong to?
After answering these questions, continue by researching the broader domain that this subdomain belongs to.
By the end of the process, you should be able to describe the development team's "path" to the tool, from the most general engineering domain and its various subproblems, all the way to the specific set of issues that makes the tool relevant.
Tips for bottom-up research
Starting points for bottom-up research can be very specific. In addition to open-source tools, consider:
Specific "paid" or "premium" features with a broader service, which typically address more niche problems
Engineering blogs describing solutions or architectural patterns for specific problems they experienced
Hacker News announcements and launches of new tools, which often contain both a justification of the project, as well as critiques and alternative solutions in the comments
Bottom-up research can be more effective than top-down research at finding interesting applications of existing tools or patterns. E.g. applying serverless functions to concurrent processes, or edge workers for AB testing.
